    "Some news you learn from the papers, and some you learn from the newsagent. Last Tuesday, when I went down to get my daily paper at the kiosk on the Place de la Croix-Rousse, I found a sheet taped to the glass, written in felt-tip in careful handwriting: \"Closing for good on June 30. Thank you for these twenty-seven years.\" I read it twice, the way you reread a break-up letter, hoping the sentence will end up saying something else.",
    "Mourad Belkacem has run this kiosk since 1997. I have always seen him there, in winter bundled up in an ageless parka, in summer in shirtsleeves, glasses pushed up on his forehead, sorting magazines with the dexterity of a croupier. He knows his customers not by their name but by their newspaper. To him, then, I am \"Le Progrès plus the Thursday crossword\", which, come to think of it, is as accurate a definition of me as any.",
    "\"What do you expect,\" he told me with a shrug, \"I sell a third of what I did ten years ago. The young read everything on their phones, the old are dying, and the ones in between don't have time any more.\" There was neither bitterness nor self-pity in his voice, only the clear-sightedness of a shopkeeper who knows how to count. Still, the figure hit me like a slap. For after all, I am one of the people who write these papers that nobody reads any more. I make my living, in short, from what is slowly killing him.",
    "I could have launched, like so many colleagues, into a meditation on the end of paper, the smell of ink, the rustle of pages on the metro. I will refrain. Nostalgia is a luxury for people who have lost nothing. Mourad doesn't miss paper: he misses people. \"What I'll miss is the comings and goings,\" he admitted. \"Do you know how many people say hello to me every morning? About two hundred. Where am I going to find two hundred hellos when I'm retired?\"",
    "That is when I understood that this kiosk had never really been a newsagent's. It was an observation post, a lost-property office, occasionally a dating agency. Mourad kept people's keys when they went on holiday, showed lost tourists the way to the market, knew who was ill and who had just become a grandfather. He was, for the neighbourhood, what newspapers claim to be for the country: a place where you find out what is going on.",
    "I asked what the town hall planned to do with the little structure. Nothing, it seems: it would be dismantled in the autumn. But the neighbourhood is resourceful. The very next day, a petition was going round at the baker's. A bookseller on the Grande-Rue proposed putting a book-exchange box in it, a retired man from the Rue d'Austerlitz suggested a meeting point for isolated neighbours. Someone, finally, had the obvious idea: that Mourad should staff it two mornings a week, as a volunteer, to hand out books and hellos.",
    "He laughed at first, then said he would think about it, which, from an adopted Lyonnais, is almost a yes. I don't know whether the project will see the light of day; town halls have their slowness and kiosks their regulations. But I know one thing. The day no one sells a single newspaper on this square any more, we will still need a place where someone asks you, as they hand you your change, whether your daughter passed her baccalauréat.",
    "Until then, I will buy my paper until June 30, crossword included. And I invite you, dear readers, to do the same, if you are lucky enough to still have a kiosk on your street corner. Not out of charity, nor even out of loyalty to paper, but because some hellos can't be downloaded.",
